    "result_text": "All tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `sharesHousehold` / `householdId` feature in the Spring Boot PetClinic REST app. The codebase already had the *rejection* half of household logic (blocking a duplicate lastName+address owner unless `sharesHousehold` was set); this change adds the *identifier* half.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `householdId` string property to the `Owner` response schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it and it's returned by `GET`/`POST /api/owners`).\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a `householdId` field (`@Column(name = \"household_id\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct maps it automatically to/from `OwnerDto`.\n\n3. **Schema SQL** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 Added the `household_id` column to the `owners` table. Tests run under the hsqldb profile with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must exist in `schema.sql`.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 \n   - `addOwner` now calls `assignHousehold(owner, ownerFieldsDto)` before saving.\n   - `assignHousehold`: when `sharesHousehold == true`, derives a stable `householdId` and sets it on the new owner, then updates any existing household mates that don't yet carry it \u2014 so the whole household shares one value.\n   - `householdIdFor`: derives a deterministic `HH-<16 hex>` id from SHA-256 of the normalized `lastName|address` (same case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed normalization used for household matching), guaranteeing every member of a household resolves to the identical id regardless of creation order.\n   - Refactored `findHouseholdMate` to delegate to a new `findHouseholdMates` (returns all mates), reusing the single household-identity definition.\n\nThe new `coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` acceptance test passes: two owners created with `sharesHousehold=true` at the same lastName+address receive the same non-blank `householdId`.",
